1.1.text:
CHAPTER IV

DUTIES AND OBLIGATIONS OF THE REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

Article 5

1. The Regional Government has the duty to undertake prevention, handling and recovery efforts for victims of violence against Women and Children, by:a. collecting data and information about Women and Children Victims of Violence;
b. disseminating laws and regulations related to the protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;
c. conducting education on anti-violence values against children and women; andd. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection of Women and Children victims of violence.
2. The Regional Government is obliged to undertake prevention, handling and recovery efforts for victims of violence against Women and Children, providing and organizing services for victims in the form of:a. providing and facilitating the establishment of integrated services for victims by involving community institutions and elements;
b. encouraging public awareness about the importance of protection for victims; andc. empowering communities for the prevention of acts of violence.

REGENT OF WEST BANGKA

REGIONAL REGULATION OF WEST BANGKA REGENCY

NUMBER 13 OF 2013

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION

FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF WEST BANGKA,

Considering:
a. that children and women are creatures of God Almighty who need to receive protection from violence for the sake of their dignity as human beings;
b. that based on the consideration as referred to in letter a, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence;

In view of:
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 5 of 1998 concerning Ratification of the Convention Against Torture and Other Cruel, Inhuman or Degrading Treatment or Punishment (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1998 Number 164, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3783);
5. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
6. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
7. Law Number 20 of 2003 concerning National Education System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 78, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4301);
8. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
9. Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 125, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 12 of 2008 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 59, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
10. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
11. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
12. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3495);
13. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
14. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning Division of Government Affairs Between the Government, Provincial Regional Government and Regency/Municipal Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
15. Regional Regulation of West Bangka Regency Number 2 of 2008 concerning Authority of West Bangka Regency (Regional Gazette of West Bangka Regency of 2008 Number 1 Series D);
16. Regional Regulation of West Bangka Regency Number 15 of 2011 concerning Cooperation in Implementation of Protection for Women and Children Victims of Acts of Violence (Regional Gazette of West Bangka Regency of 2011 Number 2 Series E);

With Mutual Approval of THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF WEST BANGKA REGENCY  and

CHAPTER VI

INSTITUTION ORGANIZING PROTECTION FOR VICTIMS

Article 8

1. The Regional Government establishes an Integrated Service Center (PPT) as the Organizing, Prevention and Protection Institution for Women and Children Victims of Violence.
2. The PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of elements of:a. health;
b. law enforcement;
c. social and empowerment;
d. education and religion;
e. Non-Governmental Organizations; andf. private sector.
3. Further provisions regarding the PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 11

1. Forms of protection provided to Victims organized by the PPT are in the form of protection:a. medical;
b. legal;
c. medicolegal (Forensic Medicine);
d. psychological; ore. economic.
2. The form of medical protection for Victims as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a, is in the form of treatment and recovery from physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics.
3. The form of legal protection for Victims as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, is in the form of services and assistance to help victims undergo legal and judicial processes.
4. The form of medicolegal protection for Victims as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, is in the form of medical services for the purposes of evidence in the field of law.
5. The form of psychological protection for Victims as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, is in the form of assistance in order to restore traumatic conditions including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from various threats and intimidation for victims and providing psychological support so that victims have self-confidence, strength and independence in problem solving.
6. The form of economic protection for victims as referred to in paragraph (1), is in the form of services for skills and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 4

Every victim has the right to receive:
a. protection;
b. information;
c. optimal service;
d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. psychological and legal assistance; andg. guarantee of rights related to the victim's status as a family member or community member.

